# Golden vectors for the SMTP worked example.
#
# Written by `smtplib.SMTP.putcmd`, captured by standing a recorder in for
# the socket, so the bytes are Python's rather than typed here:
#
#   s.putcmd("MAIL", "FROM:<a@b.invalid>")
#       ->  b'MAIL FROM:<a@b.invalid>\r\n'
#
# Only the commands that carry an argument, which is the boundary the schema
# states rather than one these vectors discovered: `command.verb` scans
# `until " "`, and `DATA`, `QUIT` and `RSET` have no space at all. Running
# `situc verify` over all five is what the note beside `struct command`
# predicts -- three conform and two are refused with
#
#     ConstraintError: command.verb has no b' ': the frame stops first
#
# which is the documented limit behaving as documented. They are left out
# rather than wished into the file: a vector the decoder refuses is a
# disagreement between a vector and a decoder, and `test_owned` fails on one
# now rather than skipping.
#
# `reply_line` gets none. It is what a SERVER writes, and there is no
# independent SMTP server on this machine to write one -- `smtpd` left the
# standard library in 3.12 and aiosmtpd is not installed. Hand-typing three
# digits and a separator would be this file agreeing with itself, which is
# the one thing a corpus must not be.
#
# The domains are `.invalid`, which RFC 2606 reserves so that a name in a
# committed file can never resolve.

command ehlo 45 48 4C 4F 20 73 69 74 75 2E 69 6E 76 61 6C 69 64 0D 0A
command mail 4D 41 49 4C 20 46 52 4F 4D 3A 3C 61 40 62 2E 69 6E 76 61 6C 69 64 3E 0D 0A
command rcpt 52 43 50 54 20 54 4F 3A 3C 63 40 64 2E 69 6E 76 61 6C 69 64 3E 0D 0A
